BUILD
Preferred one-command analysis + build + tests:
  ./compile.sh
Static/architecture analysis only:
  ./compile.sh --analyze-only
Release:
  ./compile.sh --release
Experimental chunk/group sizes:
  ./compile.sh --chunk 32 --group 16 --clean
Build, test and run:
  ./compile.sh --run

Manual fallback:
  cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
  cmake --build build --parallel
  ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

For autonomous/local coding agents: do not install or modify OS packages. If a
system dependency is missing, stop and report it. Project-local third_party
clones/builds are allowed. This restriction is an agent safety rule, not a
limitation of external build/test harnesses explicitly authorized by the user.

MANDATORY END-OF-RUN GATE
- python3 tools/architecture_check.py --root .
- normal completion: ./compile.sh (includes clang-tidy + build + tests)
- never claim DONE if architecture/static analysis/build/tests have not run;
- if clang-tidy or another required system tool is missing, STOP and report it; do not install it.

ARCHITECTURE INVARIANTS
- SDL3 owns native window and event polling. NO SDL_Renderer.
- OgreNext is attached to SDL's window and is the only renderer.
- ESC and window close must always produce a clean normal shutdown.
- Chunks are cubic 3D chunks; X/Y/Z are equal peers.
- Chunk edge and group edge are compile-time experiment knobs (16 or 32 etc.).
- ChunkGroups are sparse coordinate/world regions, never dense voxel slabs.
- AIR=runtime block id 0, but loaded AIR != unloaded chunk.
- Continuous positions are ALWAYS hierarchical: GroupCoord int64 + local cell int32 + sub-block float.
- Ogre/physics backends only receive coordinates relative to a nearby integer anchor; never absolute world floats.
- Group ownership is sticky/hysteretic, never nearest-centre flipping.
- ChunkGroup != Jolt PhysicsSystem.
- Worldgen is deterministic, data-driven, and preserves full 64-bit block/chunk ownership; Lua receives exact int64 coordinates before script scaling.
- Block visuals/materials are JSON-driven: texture -> color -> diagnostic fallback; PBS roughness/reflection/transparency/normal maps/shadow flags live in JSON.
- Greedy meshes use local repeat UVs; do not reintroduce packed-atlas TAM_WRAP.

CURRENT RENDER PATH
Greedy mesh -> quads carry BlockId -> one ManualObject material section per block
type -> individual repeatable block texture/material. This replaced the broken
packed atlas that caused the screenshot texture confetti. Empty chunks skip
meshing in O(1), and intra-chunk visibility checks read dense voxels directly.
Prototype sky clear colour is daylight blue #78A7FF. Terrain now uses HLMS PBS with a directional sun, stronger hemispherical ambient fill, PSSM shadows, JSON PBR properties and tangent-space normal-map support. A camera-mounted flashlight is enabled by default and toggled with F; Forward3D is enabled for local non-shadowed lights.

CONTENT DATA
- data/blocks.json: block mechanics + texture/color visual fields + semantic worldgen tags
- data/biomes.json: biome metadata + ACTIVE terrain profiles/mask bindings; surface/filler/resource metadata are not material-generation switches
- data/resources.json: legacy resource metadata (future ore passes are Lua+JSON field/pass data)
- data/worldgen.json: Lua scalar-field declarations + staged terrain/addon passes + decoration anchors/passes
- data/worldgen/*.lua: 2D/3D terrain/river/cave scalar fields + bounded decoration scripts
See docs/REGISTRY.md and docs/WORLDGEN.md.

CURRENT WORLDGEN
- active C++ worldgen is a generic field/pass runtime; it has no Stone/Dirt/Cave material rules;
- Lua 5.4 owns 2D/3D scalar fields and can call deterministic native noise2/noise3 helpers;
- shipped default graph currently has 30 Lua fields, 13 terrain passes, 16 addon passes, 4 anchor sets and 8 decoration passes;
- generic terrain operators: fill_below, surface_layer, surface, volume;
- biome terrain height is blended from `data/biomes.json` using data-defined mask fields;
- rivers are active generic 2D-field/pass content: continuous U/V valley profile, fixed Y=0 water reference, mountain tunnels and post-cave bed/foundation sealing;
- vegetation/trees are active data-driven decorations using scatter/column/bounded Lua structure passes;
- fields and proposal-producing passes run in parallel; merge barriers are hard-ordered terrain -> addons -> decoration, with priority then JSON order only inside each stage;
- block semantic tags drive generic replacement rules (`terrain:rock`, `terrain:carvable`, etc.);
- caves are generic `core:air` volume proposals in the addon stage; rivers/tunnels/sealing are addon passes too;
- one requested cubic chunk only; negative Y and unrestricted positive Y work;
- active streaming window remains cubic X/Y/Z;
- beaches and richer ore/resource morphology remain future content layers.
See docs/WORLDGEN.md.

KNOWN HIGH-PRIORITY GAPS
- huge-coordinate camera/render rebasing is implemented; keep the WorldPosition + render-anchor invariant intact;
- no Jolt yet;
- runFrameUpdate currently uses variable dt for camera, real fixed-step accumulator pending;
- per-block material sections are correctness-first, not final draw-call optimisation;
- dedicated ore/resource passes are not populated yet; the runtime already supports them as generic 3D volume passes;
- chunk generation is asynchronous on a background streaming coordinator and WorldGen has its own persistent field worker pool; ready-chunk commits and Ogre mesh/upload work are still main-thread budgeted, so further parallel/bounded pipeline scaling remains useful;
- user window size/fullscreen, camera speed/sensitivity, chunk render distance, commit budget, Ogre backend/options, clip distances, shadow distance and Forward3D are persisted in `~/.config/Clonecraft/settings.json`;
- sky/ambient/sun/flashlight/PSSM tuning still contains renderer-side constants and is the main remaining render-settings data-ownership gap.

